--TEST--
`Core\Heap<T>`'s `count`, `isEmpty`, `peek` and its `foreach` cursor agree at every step of a push/pop sweep, counted rather than read off a line — docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 9, `rule:classes/comparable`, `rule:iteration/foreach-subjects`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Heap::count, Core\Heap::isEmpty, Core\Heap::peek
// § 9 gives the class five members and an iteration, and four of them are
// independent readers of one question: how much is in there, and what comes
// out next. `count` reads the entries array, `isEmpty` reads its length,
// `peek` reads position 0, and the cursor `rule:iteration/foreach-subjects` builds walks a *copy*
// of the entries in pop order. Each of them could grow its own idea of the
// size and still look right on its own line — the other cases here print what
// each answered, so this one asserts only that they **agree**, and asserts it
// as a count over a whole sweep rather than as a row per step.
final class Sweep {
    // A positive entry is a push of that value; `0` is a pop, guarded so a pop
    // against an empty heap is a step of the sweep rather than the throw
    // `heap-holds-every-push-and-refuses-an-empty-read.nvst` already pins.
    public static function run(Core\Heap<int> $h, array<int> $ops): int {
        int $size = 0;
        int $agreed = 0;
        foreach ($ops as int $op) {
            if ($op == 0) {
                if (!$h->isEmpty()) {
                    $h->pop();
                    $size = $size - 1;
                }
            } else {
                $h->push($op);
                $size = $size + 1;
            }

            // `peek` is a reader that answers by throwing on an empty heap, so
            // it is read into a sentinel no row of the table can produce.
            int $peeked = -1;
            try {
                $peeked = $h->peek();
            } catch (Throwable $emptyRead) {
                $peeked = -1;
            }

            // The cursor is the fourth reader: its length and its first
            // element are the same two facts, derived from the copy it made.
            int $walked = 0;
            int $head = -1;
            foreach ($h as int $seen) {
                if ($walked == 0) {
                    $head = $seen;
                }
                $walked = $walked + 1;
            }

            if (($h->count() as int) == $size) {
                $agreed = $agreed + 1;
            }
            if ($h->isEmpty() == ($size == 0)) {
                $agreed = $agreed + 1;
            }
            if (($peeked == -1) == ($size == 0)) {
                $agreed = $agreed + 1;
            }
            if ($walked == $size) {
                $agreed = $agreed + 1;
            }
            if ($head == $peeked) {
                $agreed = $agreed + 1;
            }
        }
        return $agreed;
    }
}

// Thirteen steps whose sizes run 1,2,1,2,3,2,1,2,1,0,0,1,0 — so the sweep
// reaches the empty heap twice in the middle, takes a pop there that does
// nothing, and grows again afterwards. Every reader is asked at all thirteen.
array<int> $ops = [5, 3, 0, 8, 1, 0, 0, 9, 0, 0, 0, 4, 0];

// The ordering in force is not one of the four readers' business: the same
// sweep over `rule:classes/comparable`'s natural order and over a comparator has to produce the
// same agreement count, 5 readers x 13 steps.
var $rising = new Core\Heap<int>();
var $falling = new Core\Heap<int>(fn (int $a, int $b): int => $b - $a);
echo "agreements ", Sweep::run($rising, $ops), " ", Sweep::run($falling, $ops), " of 65\n";

// And the sweep left both heaps where the table says it did, which is what
// makes the agreement count a claim about a heap that was actually worked.
echo "drained ", $rising->count(), $falling->count(), " [", $rising->isEmpty(), $falling->isEmpty(), "]\n";
--EXPECT--
agreements 65 65 of 65
drained 00 [11]
